WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.220
If a mountain warms, where does a species' comfortable temperature go?

2
00:00:08.020 --> 00:00:11.980
A species' range is the area where its populations live and reproduce.

3
00:00:13.280 --> 00:00:14.380
Temperature falls as you climb.

4
00:00:15.220 --> 00:00:19.180
So warming moves an isotherm, a line of equal temperature, uphill.

5
00:00:20.060 --> 00:00:24.260
If temperature sets the edge of a species' tolerable conditions, the edge should

6
00:00:24.260 --> 00:00:25.260
follow.

7
00:00:25.583 --> 00:00:28.563
The speed of an isotherm is called climate velocity.

8
00:00:29.463 --> 00:00:32.503
It depends on how fast the air warms, and on the lapse rate.

9
00:00:33.303 --> 00:00:35.943
The lapse rate is how fast temperature falls with height.

10
00:00:36.923 --> 00:00:41.043
Suppose a mountainside warms 0.3 °C per decade.

11
00:00:41.923 --> 00:00:46.083
Suppose temperature falls 0.6 °C per 100 metres.

12
00:00:47.103 --> 00:00:49.343
Both numbers are invented round figures.

13
00:00:50.283 --> 00:00:53.723
Divide the first by the second, and multiply by 100 metres.

14
00:00:53.723 --> 00:00:56.903
The isotherm climbs 50 metres per decade.

15
00:00:57.967 --> 00:00:59.107
Now add a species.

16
00:01:00.447 --> 00:01:05.327
Suppose it moved at the median from a 2011 synthesis of terrestrial species: 11

17
00:01:05.327 --> 00:01:06.327
metres per decade.

18
00:01:07.347 --> 00:01:09.947
11 divided by 50 is 0.22.

19
00:01:10.907 --> 00:01:14.247
So the species covers 22 percent of the isotherm's shift.

20
00:01:15.250 --> 00:01:17.850
Run it for 30 years, which is 3 decades.

21
00:01:18.830 --> 00:01:22.470
The isotherm climbs 3 times 50, or 150 metres.

22
00:01:23.430 --> 00:01:26.710
The species climbs 3 times 11, or 33 metres.

23
00:01:27.670 --> 00:01:32.950
It ends up 117 metres below the elevation that now has its former temperature.

24
00:01:34.033 --> 00:01:35.813
This holds only under assumptions.

25
00:01:36.873 --> 00:01:38.813
Warming and the lapse rate stay constant.

26
00:01:40.013 --> 00:01:41.253
Temperature alone sets the range.

27
00:01:42.093 --> 00:01:45.533
Nothing blocks the species, such as a road, a farm or a cliff.

28
00:01:46.413 --> 00:01:51.393
A real lag could reflect slow dispersal, cues other than temperature, competitors

29
00:01:51.393 --> 00:01:53.513
already upslope, or loss of forest.

30
00:01:54.393 --> 00:01:56.293
And the median hides wide variation.

31
00:01:57.093 --> 00:02:01.413
A particular species could be far ahead of the isotherm, or far behind it.

32
00:02:02.317 --> 00:02:03.837
Say a range moves uphill.

33
00:02:04.677 --> 00:02:05.877
That shows a change happened.

34
00:02:06.797 --> 00:02:10.697
Formal detection would also test it against chance and natural variation.

35
00:02:11.657 --> 00:02:12.857
It does not show the cause.

36
00:02:13.757 --> 00:02:19.437
In average elevational shifts from 43 study sites, forest loss in warmer regions

37
00:02:19.437 --> 00:02:21.777
tended to accelerate upslope movement.

38
00:02:22.537 --> 00:02:25.657
So an uphill shift is not proof that climate acted alone.

39
00:02:26.917 --> 00:02:29.897
Attribution links a change to a cause, and tests the alternatives.

40
00:02:29.897 --> 00:02:32.837
Test the alternatives before you name the cause.
